# Tests

The test layers are in `docs/design.ja.md`. This rule is how a test is written.

## Framework

- xUnit v3 with its built-in `Assert`. No assertion library, no mocking library: hand-written fakes behind the interfaces the design names.
- Tests run on Microsoft.Testing.Platform (`global.json` `test.runner`). There is no `Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk` or `xunit.runner.visualstudio`. Run one project with `dotnet test --project <path>`, and some of its tests with `dotnet test --project <path> -- --filter-method <pattern>`. Read the exit code and the count of tests run: a run that ran no test is not a pass.
- Time comes from a fake `TimeProvider` (`Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`). A test never sleeps, never waits on the wall clock, and never asserts what the clock decides.
- Keys are generated in the test or in a fixture shared by one test class, at run time. A test never reads a key or a token from the repository.
- A file a test writes for the product to read has the form the product's own tools write: the same line ends and the trailing line break. In M4d3 the test helper wrote certificate files without the line break that `elm key certify` adds, and the cloud leases, which failed on real files since M4d1, passed every test (PR #91, design review R1).
- A server test runs the server in process on a loopback port the OS picks, and stops it in the test's cleanup.
- Every run of `./build.ps1` writes its output to a file in the scratchpad, so that a failure seen once can be named.

## Structure

- `tests/<Project>.Tests` mirrors `src/<Project>`. `Foo.cs` → `FooTests.cs`, in the same folder.
- Method names: `{Target}_{Scenario}_{Expected}` in PascalCase, English.
- Each test owns its state. Tests run in parallel; a test that cannot says why in its collection definition.

## Documentation

Every `[Fact]` and `[Theory]` has XML docs in this shape, in English:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// One line: the behavior this test guards.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Preconditions:
/// - ...
///
/// Steps:
/// - ...
///
/// Expected:
/// - ...
/// </remarks>
[Fact]
public void Verify_PayloadFieldChanged_ReturnsInvalidSignature()
{
}
```

- A `[Theory]` is documented once; list what the cases cover under Preconditions or Steps.
- No custom XML tags. Link a design section when the why is not obvious.

## What a test checks

- A test stands for the whole design statement (`.cursor/rules/spec-checks.mdc`).
- Compare with an independent reference: an RFC test vector, a token produced by a reviewed earlier run, a value worked out by hand. Do not compare a function with a re-implementation of itself.
- A memo or cache key of several values is tested by changing each value alone, the others kept.
- A test of a comparison (a priority, a tie-break, the oldest lease) sets up candidates whose values differ.
- Mutation check for a guard: change the guarded term and see the test fail, then restore it by copying the file aside and back (not `git checkout`). List the mutations in the PR. Every test of a security check gets this before the PR.
- A test of one check uses an input that passes every check before it and breaks only that one. In M3a the test that signed a lease with another key also changed its `kid`, so the `kid` check rejected it first, and the mutant that removed the signature check survived; the test of the proof's `kid` against its key had the same shape (PR #16).
- A check that a later layer also refuses needs a test that observes what only that check does. In M3b the read limit of a request body was hidden by the `Content-Length` check before it, and the first-connection TLS check by the check on every connection after it; mutants of both survived until the tests sent a chunked body and asserted that no pin was saved (PR #22).
- A limit is tested exactly at it and one past it, and a `>` → `>=` mutant shows that both sides are pinned. In M3b the tests sent only the values over the limits (a version of 33 characters, 9 fingerprints, a host of 256 bytes), and the review found that nothing checked the accepted side (PR #22). A limit counted in UTF-8 bytes gets a multi-byte character in the value at the limit. In M3c the review found the accepted side missing again, for seven new limits (PR #27): before the PR, list each limit the change adds with its two tests.
- A fake clock's `Advance` fires a periodic timer once for each period it passes: advancing 30 days fires a one-minute timer about 43,000 times. Periodic work that touches a database runs one run at a time and skips the ticks that come while it runs, or each tick takes its own connection. In M4b1 the `jti` sweep used up PostgreSQL's connections (PR #61).
- A test that waits for periodic work moves the fake clock in small steps while it waits, not once. A periodic task runs one run at a time and skips the ticks that come while it runs: a run that read the clock before one long move ended finds nothing to do, and no later tick comes unless the clock moves again. In M4d3 the expiry lines never came on the PostgreSQL runner after one move of 701 seconds (PR #91); `TestService.WaitWithClockAsync` moves a minute every 10 polls.
- A test that waits for a library's background work (a key refetch, a cache refresh) polls until that work is done; it does not decide on the next request. In M5a `Microsoft.IdentityModel` refetched the JWKS in the background while it still held keys: the second request passed on the developer's machine and failed on CI (PR #105).
- A change that adds a schema migration finds every test that names the latest version or the number of migrations, in every form (`6`, `6L`, `six`, a list of versions). In M5a the list was changed and an `Assert.Equal(6L, ...)` of the rows was not, and CI failed (PR #105).
- A test against an outside service (an identity provider, a database engine) runs on the developer's machine before it is tried on CI: start the CI step's image with the same arguments in a WSL container (`wslc run`, WSL 3.0.1 or later; not Docker). In M5a the Keycloak test went to CI five times without a local run; in a WSL container the two causes (Secure cookies over http that .NET does not send, and no default roles for users of an imported realm) showed in minutes (PR #105).
- With a fake `TimeProvider`, a timeout that the product counts on that clock does not fire unless the test moves the clock. A test's teardown does not rely on it: a test that owns servers and clients disposes the clients first, while the servers still answer. In M3d each client returned its leases to a server already stopped, each return waited about 2 seconds for the refused connection on Windows, and one test took 2 minutes 20 seconds (PR #35).
- A test whose SQL or column types differ between database engines (an ID stored as text on SQLite and as `uuid` on PostgreSQL, bytes as `BLOB` and `bytea`, a subquery) is a theory over every engine. A test of the HTTP layer alone (authentication, rate limits, body shape) may run on one engine. In M4a1 the cursor, retention, and chain tests ran on SQLite only until the review asked (PR #47).
- A test that creates a machine-scope folder (an installation key, a server data folder) creates it under a folder of the test, not directly in the system's temporary folder. The machine-scope folder check refuses a parent that other users can write, and `/tmp` on Linux is such a parent; in M3c a test that passed on Windows failed on the Linux runner (PR #27).
- A mutant must build. With warnings as errors, a mutant such as `if (false)` fails to compile (CS0162), `dotnet test --no-build` then runs the previous binaries, and the mutant looks survived. Check the build's exit code before reading the test result, and write the mutant so that it compiles. The style and nullable analyzers fail a build too: in M2 `if ( (uses & ...` failed SA1008, and passing a variable the compiler sees as maybe null failed CS8604 (PR #12). Write a mutant as code that would pass review: `if (uses < KeyUses.None || ...)` instead of deleting the first operand. Deleting a whole `if` block fails too, when it leaves a parameter unused (IDE0060) or two blank lines in a row (SA1507); in M3b five such mutants did not build (PR #22). Add a condition that cannot hold instead: `if (record.Stopped && record.Count < 0)`.
- A file put back after a mutant gets a new time stamp: write its text back rather than copy the saved file over it. `Copy-Item` keeps the old time, MSBuild then skips the build, and the last mutant's binary stays in place. In M4a2 the gate after the mutation run failed on that binary, and the cause took a search to find (PR #55).
- A test that repeats an operation whose second run must change nothing (suspending a suspended license) moves the fake clock between the two runs. On a still clock, a mutant that writes the time again writes the same value and survives. In M4a3 two such mutants survived the first run (PR #59).
- A test of a row past its expiry ends the row in the database (`UPDATE ... SET expires_at`), not by moving the clock: a moved clock also fires the periodic sweep, which can remove the row before the request. In M4d1 the mutant that found expired leases survived this way (PR #83).
- A test that reads state the product's own timer also writes waits, with a bound, until the state settles at the value it expects. One read can land while the timer's request is still out. In M4b2 a count of kept `jti` read once failed on the macOS runner (PR #67).
- A mutant of a limit that survives can mean the limit is checked twice. Remove the duplicate check (`.cursor/rules/no-dead-code.mdc`) instead of adding a test. In M4b2 the length of the `retry` list was checked in two places (PR #67).
- A surviving mutant of any check asks first whether a reachable state reaches the check. When none does, remove the check instead of adding a test, and write in the design why it cannot fail. In M4c2 the activation ID compared on an imported refresh answer could not differ, since everything that replaces the kept activation also drops the waiting request (PR #75). The search includes the states that other modes or other clients sharing the same files or folder can make. In M4d2 the check in a hosted client's `ResetServerPin` was removed as unreachable, and the review found the state that reaches it: an on-premises client's pin in the same folder (PR #87).
- Collections are compared with their own `Assert.Equal`, never inside a tuple: a tuple compares an array by reference, so the same items read as different. In M4c2 a round-trip test of two lists failed this way with equal contents (PR #75).
- A test or measurement that sends many requests to a service on a fake clock moves the clock between them. The service's rate limits refill on its own clock, so on a still clock they stop the run with `429`. In M4c2 a measurement of 200 offline requests stopped this way (PR #75).
- IDs made on a fake clock at one instant are not in creation order: UUID version 7 orders to the millisecond only. A test of a list's order checks the ID order, or moves the clock between creations. In M4a2 a feature list test expected creation order and failed at random (PR #55).
- Each error case asserts its own message, not only the exit code or the exception type. In M2 the error cases ran as a batch after the test had changed the passphrase, and the case for a server option on a standard license exited with 2 because of the passphrase, not the option; a mutant found it (PR #12).
- Before running a mutant, name an input for which it returns something else than the original. A mutant that cannot differ looks survived and proves nothing: in M1, `if (!true || !guard.Observe(...))` is the original condition, and `Equals(a, a) && !x` is `!x` (PR #8). In M4d1 `0 * await Delete(...)` still ran the delete (PR #83). Name also the test input that reaches the mutated branch: in M4d1 a mutant of the arm for a license and a feature that both expire survived, because no test license had both (PR #83).
- A script that runs mutants decides from the exit code of `dotnet test`, not from its text: Microsoft.Testing.Platform exits with 2 when a test failed, and its messages follow the OS language.
- Read the state you assert before a shared helper's cleanup runs. An assertion after a helper that resets state checks the helper, not the code.
